course: dictionary
lesson: 9252
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. The word souffle can name a food. A soufflé is a light, fluffy baked dish made from beaten egg whites. It puffs up high in the oven, like a little cloud.
explain: The word comes from French, and it means "puffed up" or "breathed". That is why a French party can use it in another way: «Souffle les bougies!» means blow out the candles. Here the word is about blowing air, not about cooking.
explain: Look for clues to decide which meaning fits. If the sentence mentions eggs, an oven or cheese, it means the dish. If it mentions candles or blowing, it means blow. Try each meaning in the sentence and see which one makes sense.
fact: A soufflé is a light, fluffy baked dish made with beaten egg whites.
fact: Soufflés can be savoury, such as cheese, or sweet, such as chocolate or lemon.
fact: Air beaten into egg whites expands when heated, so a soufflé rises in the oven.
fact: Soufflés are tricky: they can collapse quickly if you open the oven or wait too long.
fact: Souffle is French and means "puffed up" or "breathed", as in «Souffle les bougies!» (blow out the candles).
exercise: At a party someone shouts «Souffle les bougies!». What does souffle mean in this sentence? | A cheese dish baked in the oven | A tune for a birthday song | A sweet made of chocolate | *To blow, as in blow out the candles
exercise: Why does a soufflé rise high in the oven? | The cook keeps the oven door open | *Air beaten into egg whites expands when heated | Sugar melts and turns hard | Cheese makes it float like a balloon
exercise: Why do cooks say a soufflé is tricky? | *It collapses quickly if you open the oven or wait too long | It is always eaten cold, straight from the fridge | It can only be made with chocolate | It has no eggs in it
test: What does the French word souffle mean? | A small bird | *Puffed up or breathed | Very cold | A cooking pot
test: «The soufflé sank as soon as Mum opened the oven door.» What does sank show? | It was blown out like candles | It grew taller and taller | *It fell flat, as soufflés do when they collapse | It was sung at a party
exam: A sentence says «Souffle les bougies!» What is the best clue that souffle means blow, not a dish? | *The word bougies means candles, so the sentence is about blowing them out | Souffle always means a cake | There is an exclamation mark, so it must be a dish | The word starts with S, so it must be a food
